m switch to stdlib tracemalloc the result leak were patch depend on the larg file chunk path clickup task ciph 43 sourc id src cu ciph 43 task id ciph 43 titl find and patch pdf loader memori leak offlin status complet assigne bob prioriti normal date creat 2026 08 15 activ histori 2026 08 15 alic task creat open open the task becaus memori leak profil is need after the larg file chunk work 2026 08 23 bob tracemalloc patch complet patch pdf loader leak found via tracemalloc depend on the larg file chunk path\", \"dataset_id\": \"d13efb7c8a9a11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"e982c6b48a9a11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"med-synth/tasks/CIPH-43-Memory-Leak-Profiling.md\", \"id\": \"6885e4b3a9768baf\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.3107576177730466, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.12254955681863804, \"vector_similarity\": 0.74990976, \"dataset_name\": \"C1Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"med-synth/tasks/CIPH-43-Memory-Leak-Profiling.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"e982c6b48a9a11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"med-synth/tasks/CIPH-43-Memory-Leak-Profiling.md\", \"location\": \"med-synth/tasks/CIPH-43-Memory-Leak-Profiling.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 1050, \"chunk_count\": 1, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:42:17\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:02:18\", \"token_count\": 547, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"d13efb7c8a9a11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"**Source ID**: SRC-GDOC-50\\n\\n# Queue Design Review: Async Batch Processing — Concurrency Block\\n\\n**Owner**: Bob\\n\\n**Document Key**: DOC-2050\\n\\n**Last Updated**: 2026-09-04 (retrospective, covers 2026-08-25)\\n\\n## Worker Management Decision\\n\\nRetrospective note on the concurrency block confirmed 2026-08-25. For large-scale redaction jobs, the batch API moved from a synchronous blocking loop to an `asyncio`-driven queue: a single producer walks the file system and pushes document tasks, while a fixed pool of async workers pulls from the queue and orchestrates I/O against the redaction engine, built on top of the already-completed multi-threaded worker pool (T026).\\n\\n**Source ID**: SRC-GDOC-50\\n\\n# Queue Design Review: Async Batch Processing — Concurrency Block\\n\\n**Owner**: Bob\\n\\n**Document Key**: DOC-2050\\n\\n**Last Updated**: 2026-09-04 (retrospective, covers 2026-08-25)\\n\\n## Ordering & Race Risk\\n\\nBecause documents vary widely in size, workers finish out of order. When multiple workers append to the same shared results manifest concurrently, writes can interleave and corrupt the JSON structure.\\n\\n**Source ID**: SRC-GDOC-50\\n\\n# Queue Design Review: Async Batch Processing — Concurrency Block\\n\\n**Owner**: Bob\\n\\n**Document Key**: DOC-2050\\n\\n**Last Updated**: 2026-09-04 (retrospective, covers 2026-08-25)\\n\\n## Update: Confirmed I/O Race, Throughput Regression\\n\\nConcurrency testing on 2026-08-25 confirmed the race is real, not just theoretical: under concurrent load, the race condition measurably cuts batch throughput by roughly 10% compared to the expected async speedup, because workers end up retrying/serializing around the corrupted-write recovery path. This is now blocking further rollout of the async scheduler.\\n\\n**Source ID**: SRC-GDOC-50\\n\\n# Queue Design Review: Async Batch Processing — Concurrency Block\\n\\n**Owner**: Bob\\n\\n**Document Key**: DOC-2050\\n\\n**Last Updated**: 2026-09-04 (retrospective, covers 2026-08-25)\\n\\n## Status\\n\\nT037 is **Blocked** on resolving the manifest-write race before the async scheduler can be considered production-ready; the throughput regression it causes depends directly on how the T026 worker pool's result-writing is serialized.\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"sourc id src gdoc 50 queue design review async batch process concurr block owner bob document key doc 2050 last updat 2026 09 04 retrospect cover 2026 08 25 worker manag decis retrospect note on the concurr block confirm 2026 08 25 for larg scale redact job the batch api move from a synchron block loop to an asyncio driven queue a singl produc walk the file system and push document task while a fix pool of async worker pull from the queue and orchestr i o against the redact engin built on top of the alreadi complet multi thread worker pool t026 sourc id src gdoc 50 queue design review async batch process concurr block owner bob document key doc 2050 last updat 2026 09 04 retrospect cover 2026 08 25 order race risk becaus document vari wide in size worker finish out of order when multipl worker append to the same share result manifest concurr write can interleav and corrupt the json structur sourc id src gdoc 50 queue design review async batch process concurr block owner bob document key doc 2050 last updat 2026 09 04 retrospect cover 2026 08 25 updat confirm i o race throughput regress concurr test on 2026 08 25 confirm the race is real not just theoret under concurr load the race condit measur cut batch throughput by rough 10 compar to the expect async speedup becaus worker end up retri serial around the corrupt write recoveri path this is now block further rollout of the async schedul sourc id src gdoc 50 queue design review async batch process concurr block owner bob document key doc 2050 last updat 2026 09 04 retrospect cover 2026 08 25 status t037 is block on resolv the manifest write race befor the async schedul can be consid product readi the throughput regress it caus depend direct on how the t026 worker pool s result write is serial\", \"dataset_id\": \"d13efb7c8a9a11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"e9a578b28a9a11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"med-synth/documentation/sources/2026-09-04-Async-Batch-Processing-Queue-Review.md\", \"id\": \"188f8e49d969781d\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.30919972970832804, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.12741007101189725, \"vector_similarity\": 0.7333756, \"dataset_name\": \"C1Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"med-synth/documentation/sources/2026-09-04-Async-Batch-Processing-Queue-Review.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"e9a578b28a9a11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"med-synth/documentation/sources/2026-09-04-Async-Batch-Processing-Queue-Review.md\", \"location\": \"med-synth/documentation/sources/2026-09-04-Async-Batch-Processing-Queue-Review.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 1694, \"chunk_count\": 2, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:42:17\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:01:51\", \"token_count\": 573, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"d13efb7c8a9a11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"**Source ID**: SRC-GDOC-25\\n\\n# Design Note: Large File Chunking Strategy\\n\\n**Owner**: Charlie\\n\\n**Document Key**: DOC-2025\\n\\n**Last Updated**: 2026-08-14\\n\\n## The Failure Mode\\n\\nWhen processing massive multi-GB PDF documents (e.g., a 4,000-page merged archive), the loader attempts to load the entire document tree into RAM. For files exceeding 2GB, this consistently results in an `OOMKilled` exception in our standard 4GB Docker containers.\\n\\n**Source ID**: SRC-GDOC-25\\n\\n# Design Note: Large File Chunking Strategy\\n\\n**Owner**: Charlie\\n\\n**Document Key**: DOC-2025\\n\\n**Last Updated**: 2026-08-14\\n\\n## Chunking Implementation\\n\\nWe implemented and merged a \\\"Stream-and-Split\\\" pre-processing layer today. \\n\\n1. The CLI reads the source PDF in 100-page blocks.\\n\\n2. Each block is saved as a temporary sub-document (`doc_part_1.pdf`, `doc_part_2.pdf`).\\n\\n3. The worker pool processes these sub-documents independently.\\n\\n4. A post-processing step merges the redacted sub-documents back into a single final PDF.\\n\\n**Source ID**: SRC-GDOC-25\\n\\n# Design Note: Large File Chunking Strategy\\n\\n**Owner**: Charlie\\n\\n**Document Key**: DOC-2025\\n\\n**Last Updated**: 2026-08-14\\n\\n## Ordering and Memory Trade-off\\n\\nThis approach solves the RAM issue but introduces a significant disk I/O and ordering overhead. Re-merging the PDFs requires careful tracking of the chunk sequence. Furthermore, if a regex pattern was supposed to match across a page boundary (e.g., a sentence split across pages 100 and 101), the chunking will break the match. We are trading context-awareness for memory stability.\\n\\n*Pending*: We need to run a stress test with a 10,000-page document to verify if the final re-merge step (which also uses RAM) re-introduces the OOM crash.\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"sourc id src gdoc 25 design note larg file chunk strategi owner charli document key doc 2025 last updat 2026 08 14 the failur mode when process massiv multi gb pdf document e g a4 000 page merg archiv the loader attempt to load the entir document tree into ram for file exceed 2gb this consist result in an oomkil except in our standard 4gb docker contain sourc id src gdoc 25 design note larg file chunk strategi owner charli document key doc 2025 last updat 2026 08 14 chunk implement we implement and merg a stream and split pre process layer today 1 the cli read the sourc pdf in 100 page block 2 each block is save a a temporari sub document doc _ part _ 1 pdf doc _ part _ 2 pdf 3 the worker pool process these sub document independ 4 a post process step merg the redact sub document back into a singl final pdf sourc id src gdoc 25 design note larg file chunk strategi owner charli document key doc 2025 last updat 2026 08 14 order and memori trade off this approach solv the ram issu but introduc a signific disk i o and order overhead re merg the pdfs requir care track of the chunk sequenc furthermor if a regex pattern wa suppos to match across a page boundari e g a sentenc split across page 100 and 101 the chunk will break the match we are trade context awar for memori stabil pend we need to run a stress test with a 10 000 page document to verifi if the final re merg step which also us ram re introduc the oom crash\", \"dataset_id\": \"d13efb7c8a9a11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"e9af1e808a9a11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"med-synth/documentation/sources/2026-08-14-Large-File-Chunking-Review.md\", \"id\": \"a0395252cfde12bb\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.30795353003192083, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.10310750004560121, \"vector_similarity\": 0.7859276, \"dataset_name\": \"C1Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"med-synth/documentation/sources/2026-08-14-Large-File-Chunking-Review.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"e9af1e808a9a11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"med-synth/documentation/sources/2026-08-14-Large-File-Chunking-Review.md\", \"location\": \"med-synth/documentation/sources/2026-08-14-Large-File-Chunking-Review.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 1406, \"chunk_count\": 1, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:42:17\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:01:41\", \"token_count\": 447, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"d13efb7c8a9a11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"**Source ID**: SRC-GDOC-44\\n\\n# Profiling Report: PDF Loader Memory Leak — Tracemalloc Patch\\n\\n**Owner**: Bob\\n\\n**Document Key**: DOC-2044\\n\\n**Last Updated**: 2026-08-23\\n\\n## Trigger\\n\\nProfiling was opened after the T031 large-file chunking reopen surfaced memory growth on 2GB+ documents; the team wanted to confirm whether the loader itself, separate from the chunk-buffer issue being fixed under T031, was also retaining memory it shouldn't.\\n\\n**Source ID**: SRC-GDOC-44\\n\\n# Profiling Report: PDF Loader Memory Leak — Tracemalloc Patch\\n\\n**Owner**: Bob\\n\\n**Document Key**: DOC-2044\\n\\n**Last Updated**: 2026-08-23\\n\\n## Tooling Constraint\\n\\nThe offline lab environment does not have the team's usual commercial profiler available (network-gated license check), so profiling switched to Python's stdlib `tracemalloc`, which works fully offline.\\n\\n**Source ID**: SRC-GDOC-44\\n\\n# Profiling Report: PDF Loader Memory Leak — Tracemalloc Patch\\n\\n**Owner**: Bob\\n\\n**Document Key**: DOC-2044\\n\\n**Last Updated**: 2026-08-23\\n\\n## Findings and Patch\\n\\nTracemalloc traced the retained allocations to the PDF loader's internal page-object cache, which was holding references to fully-parsed page objects long after the redaction pass for that page had completed. Bob patched the loader to explicitly drop the cache entry once a page's redaction pass finishes, rather than waiting for document close.\\n\\n**Source ID**: SRC-GDOC-44\\n\\n# Profiling Report: PDF Loader Memory Leak — Tracemalloc Patch\\n\\n**Owner**: Bob\\n\\n**Document Key**: DOC-2044\\n\\n**Last Updated**: 2026-08-23\\n\\n## Validation\\n\\nRe-ran the same profiling pass post-patch: peak memory during a 2GB+ document run dropped to match the chunk-bounded footprint expected from the T031 chunking work, confirming the loader-side leak — distinct from the T031 buffer-release issue — is resolved.\\n\\n*Status*: Completed. Loader-side leak patched and verified; result depends on and complements the T031 chunking/buffer-release fix.\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"sourc id src gdoc 44 profil report pdf loader memori leak tracemalloc patch owner bob document key doc 2044 last updat 2026 08 23 trigger profil wa open after the t031 larg file chunk reopen surfac memori growth on 2gb document the team want to confirm whether the loader itself separ from the chunk buffer issu be fix under t031 wa also retain memori it shouldn t sourc id src gdoc 44 profil report pdf loader memori leak tracemalloc patch owner bob document key doc 2044 last updat 2026 08 23 tool constraint the offlin lab environ doe not have the team s usual commerci profil avail network gate licens check so profil switch to python s stdlib tracemalloc which work fulli offlin sourc id src gdoc 44 profil report pdf loader memori leak tracemalloc patch owner bob document key doc 2044 last updat 2026 08 23 find and patch tracemalloc trace the retain alloc to the pdf loader s intern page object cach which wa hold refer to fulli pars page object long after the redact pas for that page had complet bob patch the loader to explicit drop the cach entri onc a page s redact pas finish rather than wait for document close sourc id src gdoc 44 profil report pdf loader memori leak tracemalloc patch owner bob document key doc 2044 last updat 2026 08 23 valid re ran the same profil pas post patch peak memori dure a 2gb document run drop to match the chunk bound footprint expect from the t031 chunk work confirm the loader side leak distinct from the t031 buffer releas issu is resolv status complet loader side leak patch and verifi result depend on and complement the t031 chunk buffer releas fix\", \"dataset_id\": \"d13efb7c8a9a11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"e9df68608a9a11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"med-synth/documentation/sources/2026-08-23-Memory-Leak-Profiling-Report.md\", \"id\": \"f22e8883670ff4bd\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.30155260977304665, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.12254955681863804, \"vector_similarity\": 0.7192264, \"dataset_name\": \"C1Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"med-synth/documentation/sources/2026-08-23-Memory-Leak-Profiling-Report.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"e9df68608a9a11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"med-synth/documentation/sources/2026-08-23-Memory-Leak-Profiling-Report.md\", \"location\": \"med-synth/documentation/sources/2026-08-23-Memory-Leak-Profiling-Report.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 1441, \"chunk_count\": 1, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:42:17\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:01:00\", \"token_count\": 490, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"d13efb7c8a9a11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}], \"pagination\": {\"page\": 1, \"page_size\": 10, \"total_chunks\": 70, \"total_pages\": 7}, \"query_info\": {\"question\": \"CIPH-41 large-file chunking September 22 2026 status reopening owner next action regression test completion\", \"similarity_threshold\": 0.2, \"vector_weight\": 0.3, \"keyword_search\": false, \"dataset_count\": 1}}"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
